## Summary {.unnumbered}

::: callout-note
## Today you learned

1.  `brms` fits regression with `outcome ~ predictor` — same call as
    last chapter, one more term.
2.  Ask **how much**, not **whether**. The magnitude and its
    uncertainty are what a decision needs.
3.  **Centre your predictors** so the intercept means something — and
    so the prior you set is the quantity you read back.
4.  A **slope** is a posterior distribution; report its **credible
    interval**, and plot the bundle of lines it implies.
5.  **Parameter uncertainty** (the line, `add_epred_draws()`) shrinks
    with more data; **prediction uncertainty** (one person,
    `add_predicted_draws()`) largely doesn't.
6.  A **posterior predictive check** shows whether the model
    reproduces the data — necessary, not sufficient.
7.  Classical `lm()` and Bayesian regression usually agree closely on
    the point estimate — the posterior is the added value.
:::

